The map stores how many times every number appears. After finding the highest count, I find the largest count that is still smaller than it. Among numbers with that count, the smaller number wins.

A single distinct value, or a list where every distinct value has the same count, has no valid second frequency.

```java
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

class Solution {
    public int secondMostFrequentElement(int[] nums) {
        Map<Integer, Integer> frequency = new HashMap<>();

        for (int value : nums) {
            frequency.put(value, frequency.getOrDefault(value, 0) + 1);
        }

        int highest = 0;
        for (int count : frequency.values()) {
            highest = Math.max(highest, count);
        }

        int secondFrequency = 0;
        for (int count : frequency.values()) {
            if (count < highest) {
                secondFrequency = Math.max(secondFrequency, count);
            }
        }

        if (secondFrequency == 0) {
            return -1;
        }

        int answer = Integer.MAX_VALUE;
        for (Map.Entry<Integer, Integer> entry : frequency.entrySet()) {
            if (entry.getValue() == secondFrequency) {
                answer = Math.min(answer, entry.getKey());
            }
        }

        return answer == Integer.MAX_VALUE ? -1 : answer;
    }
}
```

The input array is not changed. The map also works when values are negative or very large.

Time complexity: `O(n + k)`, with `k` distinct values.
Space complexity: `O(k)`.

I counted each value first, then ordered the values by frequency. The largest frequency is skipped, and the smallest value with the next lower frequency is returned.

If all values have the same frequency, there is no second frequency level, so the function returns `-1`.

```cpp
#include <algorithm>
#include <unordered_map>
#include <vector>

using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    int secondMostFrequentElement(vector<int>& nums) {
        unordered_map<int, int> frequency;
        for (int value : nums) {
            frequency[value]++;
        }

        vector<pair<int, int>> values;
        for (const auto& entry : frequency) {
            values.push_back({entry.second, entry.first});
        }

        sort(values.begin(), values.end(), [](const pair<int, int>& a,
                                               const pair<int, int>& b) {
            if (a.first != b.first) {
                return a.first > b.first;
            }
            return a.second < b.second;
        });

        if (values.size() < 2) {
            return -1;
        }

        int highestFrequency = values[0].first;
        for (const auto& item : values) {
            if (item.first < highestFrequency) {
                return item.second;
            }
        }

        return -1;
    }
};
```

For example, counts of `4, 4, 2, 2, 2, 7` are 2, 3, and 1. The highest count is 3, so the answer is `4`, which has the next lower count.

Time complexity: `O(n + k log k)`, where `k` is the number of distinct values.
Space complexity: `O(k)`.

This is the slower version, but the comparison is easy to follow. I first collect distinct values. For every pair of values, I count their appearances and keep the best two frequency levels.

The first level is the highest count. A candidate for the answer must have a smaller count than that level. If several candidates share the second count, the smaller number is kept.

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.HashSet;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Set;

class Solution {
    public int secondMostFrequentElement(int[] nums) {
        Set<Integer> distinct = new HashSet<>();
        for (int value : nums) {
            distinct.add(value);
        }

        if (distinct.size() < 2) {
            return -1;
        }

        List<Integer> values = new ArrayList<>(distinct);
        int highest = 0;

        for (int value : values) {
            highest = Math.max(highest, countOf(nums, value));
        }

        int secondFrequency = 0;
        for (int value : values) {
            int count = countOf(nums, value);
            if (count < highest) {
                secondFrequency = Math.max(secondFrequency, count);
            }
        }

        if (secondFrequency == 0) {
            return -1;
        }

        int answer = Integer.MAX_VALUE;
        for (int value : values) {
            if (countOf(nums, value) == secondFrequency) {
                answer = Math.min(answer, value);
            }
        }

        return answer == Integer.MAX_VALUE ? -1 : answer;
    }

    private int countOf(int[] nums, int target) {
        int count = 0;
        for (int value : nums) {
            if (value == target) {
                count++;
            }
        }
        return count;
    }
}
```

For `[3, 3, 8, 8, 8, 1, 1]`, the counts are 2, 3, and 2. The highest count is 3, so the second count is 2. Both `1` and `3` qualify, and the smaller value `1` is returned.

Time complexity: `O(nk)`, where `k` is the number of distinct values.
Space complexity: `O(k)`.

Here the frequencies are grouped into buckets. Bucket `f` contains all values that occur `f` times. I inspect the buckets from high to low, skip the first non-empty one, and choose the smallest value in the next non-empty bucket.

```cpp
#include <algorithm>
#include <unordered_map>
#include <vector>

using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    int secondMostFrequentElement(vector<int>& nums) {
        unordered_map<int, int> frequency;
        for (int value : nums) {
            frequency[value]++;
        }

        vector<vector<int>> buckets(nums.size() + 1);
        for (const auto& entry : frequency) {
            buckets[entry.second].push_back(entry.first);
        }

        bool skippedHighest = false;
        for (int count = static_cast<int>(nums.size()); count >= 1; count--) {
            if (buckets[count].empty()) {
                continue;
            }

            if (!skippedHighest) {
                skippedHighest = true;
                continue;
            }

            return *min_element(buckets[count].begin(), buckets[count].end());
        }

        return -1;
    }
};
```

The bucket array is sized by `nums.size()`, since no value can appear more times than the length of the input. If the array is empty or there is only one non-empty frequency bucket, `-1` is returned.

Time complexity: `O(n + k)`, ignoring the small scan through empty buckets.
Space complexity: `O(n + k)`.

